the nfl was planning to play this fall  that's our hope and that's our planning  uh today  we can help our country heal we can help  bring our communities together we can  provide hope we can provide a  distraction from the everyday issues and  show people that  there is a future out there and that  we're all going to be part of that  that's the first we've seen and heard  directly from roger goodell since the  pandemic began last month and it popped  up kind of without much fanfare on the  nfl's instagram account peter and i i  think it's useful at times like this to  hear from people like roger goodell the  guy who runs the most popular sport in  america there are a lot of  impressionable people out there that are  getting mixed messages and to the extent  that you can provide a message of hope a  message of positivity i think it's very  important i'm glad that roger goodell  did it but  every time i hear  the nfl is planning to play  i understand that we want to project  optimism and hope i just think the more  accurate statement is we're planning for  every possible scenario and i hope they  are i hope they aren't simply planning  to play but planning for everything that  can happen because one thing we've  learned over the past month  anything can happen and anything has  happened in the past four weeks  well mike you know we're all trying to  uh  you know divine what exactly is going to  happen going into the future  and so and i think we're all somewhat  anyway we're all these days at least  kind of news junkies  and and so the other night i was  watching the pbs newshour judy woodruff  and she has an interview with bill gates  and bill gates obviously has got a lot  of  experience with his philanthropy in  africa and around the world with  vaccines he's he's  he basically become an expert in this  field  and he he said in so many words that  when judy woodruff asked him about  big gatherings of people and conventions  at conventions or or stadiums  and bill gates said essentially that you  know he does not think it's a very good  idea until  there is a vaccine  and so until  if you take that a step further  i i don't hear anybody saying yep by  november we'll have a vaccine i mean  and again  all i'm doing is trying to spitball just  like we all are and sometimes that's a  little bit dangerous  but my feeling is that it's unrealistic  to think  that you know on september 10th when the  kansas city chiefs if the kansas city  chiefs open the season at arrowhead  stadium it's unrealistic to not even  consider  that there might not be fans in the  stands and i've talked to a few players  this week who've openly talked about the  fact that we're probably going to play  some games without fans in the stands  and so i think the sooner people start  to kind of wrap their head around that i  think the sort of the better and the  more realistic it will be for people  trying to anticipate the future  i agree with you completely and the  problem is when you speak like that  there are people who get riled up and  say you're being an alarmist you're  being unrealistic well we're being  realistic and we don't want to create  false hope there's nothing wrong with  hope  but i think it's detrimental to fill  people's heads up with ideas that don't  come to fruition because then peter it's  just one disappointment after another  anybody who was looking forward to  watching ufc 249 finds out last night  that's not going to happen people  looking forward to seeing baseball in  june they're going to be disappointed if  they find out that's not going to happen  so i think the hope needs to be  realistic and everyone needs to be  pragmatic and and it is a very simple  chain of logic it's going to be  impossible to get back to where we were  without a vaccine for this virus period  and the big concern is we alluded to  last segment wave two wave three the  news last night told me that wave three  of the spanish flu was the worst wave  that we thought we were over it twice  and people let the guard down and it  came back worse the third time so until  there's a vaccine i agree with you the  idea of full stadiums or anyone in  stadiums seems incomprehensible but but  it's still important for the people who  run our private institutions to provide  messages of hope and i was inspired by  the way that roger goodell closed his  comments that emerged earlier this week  listen to what roger goodell had to say  and come to your own conclusion as to  whether or not you find it inspiring i  do  we're going to get through this um we're  going to get through this together we're  going to get this as a country and as a  as a world  and that um  this is something that we're going to  we're going to to overcome  and you know people should keep that  hope because uh  we're going to get back to doing the  things that we were doing and hopefully  creating a great future for so many  people and i hope everyone stays  involved and does what's best in our  communities in the short term but more  importantly keeps focusing on  the future because it's going to be a  great future for us  see it's simple to deliver that message  but it's also very important it's very  powerful because we're living in this  groundhog day existence where every  morning i got you babies playing at 6 00  a.m and we slammed our hand on the clock  and here we go with another day and we  have no idea when this is going to end  and it's important to hear from people  just that simple very basic but very  pure and true message that we are going  to find a way through this that it is  going to get back to normal at some  point however long it takes we're  climbing that mountain peter  one inch at a time and we have no idea  where the top is but when you hear  messages like that from people of high  position high level of trust and yeah  they they boo him at the draft that  sport but roger goodell is still the guy  who is at the very top of the nfl  pyramid that i think is helpful to  people and and those little messages can  help people get through  one day a rough day a bad day you hear  something like that it gets you through  that bad day so i encourage him and  others in positions like that to keep  putting those messages out there to keep  us all moving in the direction that we  all need to be moving  yeah and and mike look i i mean  i i think that everybody should  basically keep their eye on the prize  and the prize being getting back to a  normal life  and if you keep your eye on the prize  about getting back to a normal life then  that means you're not going to be  careless and you're not going to say  well i'm going to jump over this fence  and me and eight buddies are gonna go  play basketball um you know it's  interesting yesterday i was talking to  chris harris jr you know the new  cornerback of the los angeles chargers  last year his workout his workout  routine  in texas where he lives in dallas was  his trainer had about 20 to 25 people  who every day would go they do some  lifting they do some drills uh they'd be  on a a football field in dallas so this  year what they've done chris harris jr  said it sort of looks like a field day  you know around said we have five guys  who don't come close to each other and  the way the routine is they all are on  different stuff  which is obviously cleaned off and and  all that  and so all of this stuff is done  trying to keep in mind we are getting  ready  okay but we're getting ready in a  different way than we ever have before  and that is the thing to keep in mind  okay there are ways to do  some of this stuff that you've always  done but to do it in a more careful way  and to not  just be careless so to me  you can keep your eye on the prize  getting back to a normal life and you  know what some of the new normal is not  going to be what it used to be  i keep thinking to myself  you know  if i could sign right now  for a 12-game season without a buy that  starts on october 18th  and maybe for either part or all the  year there are no fans in the stands  quite honestly i'd sign for that right  now because to me what it means is that  you will play the nfl season in some  degree  of regularity you might not have fans in  the stands  but  what is the strength of the national  football league for its consumers the  televised product and i know everybody's  gonna say oh my god it won't be the same  at arrowhead stadium without the sea of  red well of course it won't  but we're not the same either and you  just have to say to yourself okay  could we have 70 percent of the  enjoyment  you know i mean and and the fans are not  there  i'd rather have 70 percent of the  enjoyment than not have games  and the bottom line is if those games  have to proceed without fans present  that means we're going to still be  subject to some degree of the  restrictions that are keeping us at home  and we're going to need those games  we're going to want those games we're  going to want that thing we can look  forward to at the end of each week to  see our favorite team play to have our  fantasy team in action whatever the case  may be it's something that breaks up  that notion that every day is the same  one day after the other like i said  earlier bill murray slamming his hand on  the clock when sonny and cher are  singing that song at 6 a.m in  punxsutawney pennsylvania we can only  take so much of that we need things to  look forward  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports
 

